  TODO 1 — Fix the timezone-dependent test (real bug, cheap)

  modules/git/pipeline/lfs_test.go:34 fails on any machine whose local offset at
  2022-12-21 is -0500 (US Eastern, most of the US east coast). Reproduced and
  confirmed:

  go test -run '^TestFindLFSFile$' ./modules/git/pipeline/   → FAIL
  TZ=UTC go test -run '^TestFindLFSFile$' ./modules/git/pipeline/   → ok

  The cause is time.Parse semantics: when the parsed offset matches the current
  Local zone's offset at that instant, Go returns a Time carrying time.Local
  instead of a fixed zone. The value from git (lfs_nogogit.go:100 /
  lfs_gogit.go:58) always carries a fixed -0500 zone. Same instant, different
  *Location pointer — and assert.Equal does a struct compare, so it fails.

  expected: ... time.Local          ← the parsed tm
  actual  : ... time.Location("")   ← git's fixed zone

  Fix — compare instants, not structs:

  assert.Equal(t, tm.UTC(), stats[0].When.UTC())

  I verified this passes with both a Local-promoted and a fixed-zone value. This
  is CI-invisible (CI runs UTC), so it only ever bites contributors — worth
  fixing precisely because the test suite is otherwise green for them.

  I checked the other five time.Parse(time.RFC3339, …) call sites in tests
  (repo_stats_test.go, schedule_spec_test.go, util_date_test.go, repo_test.go,
  models/actions): all use the parsed value as an input, not as an equality
  target. The bug is isolated to this one line.

